Requirements: 57   with obligations: 56   deliberately none: 1

[task-01] Decomposition is re-derived from scratch on every run. `rerun.py` states the
    rule it works under: a changed task invalidates everything, because obligations are
    a function of the task text. One edited character therefore discards the whole prior
    decomposition and asks the model for all of it again.
    -> rerun-rederive-from-scratch  [functional/explicit]
       Decomposition is re-derived from scratch on every run, and a changed task
       invalidates everything because obligations are a function of the task text.

[task-02] Measurement over three runs of one unchanged task file, differing only by
    seed, found no obligation content lost — and 38 distinct criterion wordings across
    three runs of roughly 20 criteria each, 8 of them appearing in all three runs and 23
    in exactly one, with identifiers re-minted alongside the wordings. The criterion
    text is itself the prompt for later stages, so that churn sets a floor under every
    downstream stability figure. It is not model nondeterminism: an identical prompt at
    temperature 0 with a fixed seed returned byte-identical output three times. What
    moves is which request the model is given.
    -> task-text-is-prompt-for-later-stages  [functional/explicit]
       The criterion text is itself the prompt for later stages, so churn in criterion
       wording sets a floor under downstream stability figures.
    -> not-model-nondeterminism  [explanation_observability/explicit]
       The observed movement is attributable to which request the model is given rather
       than model nondeterminism.
    -> criterion-churn-preserves-content  [regression/explicit]
       Across three runs of one unchanged task file differing only by seed, no
       obligation content is lost.
    -> criterion-wording-churn-across-runs  [functional/explicit]
       Across three runs of one unchanged task file differing only by seed, criterion
       wording churn occurs, with identifiers re-minted alongside the wordings.

[task-03] Make decomposition incremental over recorded state. A requirement whose text
    did not change keeps the obligations already derived from it, without asking again.
    -> incremental-decomposition-over-recorded-state  [functional/explicit]
       Make decomposition incremental over recorded state so that a requirement whose
       text did not change keeps the obligations already derived from it without asking
       again.
    -> unchanged-requirement-keeps-obligations  [invariant/explicit]
       A requirement whose text did not change keeps the obligations already derived
       from it.
    -> unchanged-requirement-skips-model-call  [functional/explicit]   (also serves constraint-01)
       A requirement whose text is unchanged from the continued run causes no model call
       during decomposition.

